The '''Okami Direwolves''' ({{nihongo|大紙ダイアウルブス|Ōkami Daiāurubusu}}) are a professional [[Lurandian Football]] team based in [[Okami]], [[Okaiken]]. Founded in 1985 as a founding member of the [[X-League]], the Direwolves are one of the league's most storied franchises, having won five [[Rice Bowl]] championships and made 22 playoff appearances. The team is owned by the [[Okami Financial Group]] and plays its home games at [[Okami National Stadium]].

The Direwolves are known for their defense-first identity, physical playing style, and deep connection to the {{nihongo|[[Cult of Iron]]|鉄身信仰|Tesshin-shinkō}} cultural movement. The franchise's defining era was the "Iron Curtain" defense of 2013–2017, anchored by middle linebacker [[Naoto Kongo]] and defensive end [[Kengo Shibata|Kengo "The Wall" Shibata]], which produced the 2015 Rice Bowl championship and established the Direwolves as the X-League's most feared defensive unit. The team's rivalry with the [[Toyozawa Ironblades]]—particularly the decade-long Shibata–[[Ryuichi Nakada|Nakada]] matchup—is the most watched regular season broadcast in Okaiken television history.

==Franchise history==
===Founding and early years (1985–1994)===
The Okami Direwolves were one of the six founding franchises of the X-League in 1985, backed by the [[Okami Financial Group]], one of Okaiken's largest banking and insurance conglomerates. The team's name and red-and-black color scheme were chosen to evoke the image of the {{nihongo|ōkami|狼|wolf}}, a creature revered in Sanese folklore as a guardian spirit and a symbol of pack loyalty and relentless pursuit.

The early Direwolves struggled, posting losing records in six of their first ten seasons. Despite the arrival of several talented players, the team lacked the defensive identity that would later define it. The franchise's first playoff appearance came in 1989, a 21–14 semifinal loss to the [[Toyozawa Ironblades]]. The 1990–1994 period was marked by erratic play and frequent coaching changes, with three head coaches cycling through the position.

===The Shibata era and first dynasty (1995–2005)===
The Direwolves' fortunes changed permanently with the arrival of defensive end [[Kengo Shibata|Kengo Shibata]] in the 1995 season. Drafted 5th overall out of [[Toyozawa University]], Shibata immediately transformed the Direwolves' pass rush, recording 10 sacks as a rookie and earning the nickname "The Wall" for his impenetrable edge presence. Under head coach [[Hiroto Kanzaki]], the Direwolves adopted a defense-first philosophy that would become the franchise's permanent identity.

Behind Shibata's pass rush and the precision passing of quarterback [[Tatsuo Kanzaki]] (no relation to the coach), the Direwolves won three consecutive Rice Bowl titles from 1997 to 1999—the X-League's first dynasty. The 1998 Rice Bowl, a 28–17 victory over the [[Saitama Forges]], remains the franchise's most dominant championship performance, with Shibata recording 4 sacks and the defense forcing 5 turnovers.

The dynasty faded in the early 2000s as Tatsuo Kanzaki aged and the offense stagnated, but the Direwolves remained competitive, making the playoffs in 2001, 2003, and 2005. Shibata's individual dominance continued unabated—he won his first Defensive Player of the Year award in 2002—and the franchise's defensive reputation attracted free agents and draft picks who wanted to play alongside the league's most feared defender.

===Transition and rebuilding (2006–2011)===
The post-Kanzaki era was difficult. The Direwolves cycled through quarterbacks—[[Shinji Maeda]], [[Koji Asakura]], [[Ren Takahashi]]—none of whom could replicate the consistency and field leadership that had defined the dynasty years. The defense remained elite, anchored by the aging but still devastating Shibata, but the offense ranked in the bottom half of the league for five consecutive seasons.

The [[Jutsu Restoration]] of 2011 brought a cultural shift that benefited the Direwolves disproportionately. The {{nihongo|Forge State|鍛冶国家|Kaji Kokka}} ideology's emphasis on physical dominance and defensive discipline aligned perfectly with the franchise's identity, and the surge in X-League viewership and investment coincided with the Direwolves' return to competitiveness.

===The Iron Curtain era (2012–2019)===
The 2012 X-League Draft was the turning point. With the 3rd overall pick, the Direwolves selected middle linebacker [[Naoto Kongo]] out of [[Okami Teikoku University]]. The pairing of Kongo with Shibata created the "Iron Curtain" defense—the most feared defensive unit in X-League history.

Under defensive coordinator [[Hisashi Morimoto]], the Iron Curtain operated on a simple premise: Shibata commanded double-teams on the edge, freeing Kongo to diagnose and destroy in the middle. The Direwolves' defense allowed a league-low 187 points in 2013, Kongo's second season, and the team made the playoffs for the first time since 2008.

The pinnacle came in the [[2015 X-League season|2015 season]]. The Direwolves posted a 12–2 record and advanced to the [[2016 Rice Bowl]] against the heavily favored [[Toyozawa Ironblades]], led by star quarterback [[Ryuichi Nakada]]. Kongo's defensive game plan—calling every signal from the middle—held Nakada to his lowest rushing output of the season, and the Direwolves won 17–14. Kongo was named Rice Bowl MVP, the first middle linebacker to receive the honor.

The Iron Curtain dominated through 2017, with Kongo winning Defensive Player of the Year in 2013, 2016, and 2017. The 2016 season was particularly notable: Kongo recorded 7 sacks in 12 games and delivered a legendary 17-tackle, 3-sack performance against the [[Fukuoka Kraken]] in Week 4.

Kongo's retirement in January 2020—following a 2018 labrum injury and a declining 2019 season—ended the Iron Curtain era. The Direwolves retired his No. 52 jersey in a ceremony before the 2020 season opener, the first number retirement in franchise history.

===Modern era (2020–present)===
Despite losing Kongo, the Direwolves remained competitive. The 2020 season saw the team win the Rice Bowl with a 34–14 victory over the [[Toyozawa Ironblades]], powered by Shibata's continued dominance and the emergence of quarterback [[Kaito Mizushima]]. The 2024 season brought another Rice Bowl title—a 27–20 victory over the [[Saitama Samurai]]—with the defense again carrying the team.

The Shibata–Nakada rivalry continued to define the franchise's narrative, with the annual matchup drawing record television audiences. The 2024 regular season meeting between the Direwolves and Ironblades attracted 52.3 million viewers, the most-watched regular season broadcast in Okaiken television history for any sport.

The 2025 season ended with a Rice Bowl loss to the Ironblades, 31–24, but the Direwolves' core remains intact and the franchise is widely considered a perennial contender.

==Identity and culture==
===Team colors and uniform===
The Direwolves' team colors are '''red, black, and white'''. Red represents the forge fire and the blood of competition; black represents the iron and the defense-first identity; white serves as an accent color. The home uniform features a red jersey with black numbers and white trim, black pants with red stripes, and a red helmet with the Direwolves' wolf-head logo. The away uniform is white with red numbers and black trim.

In 2014, the Direwolves introduced an alternate "Iron" uniform—an all-black jersey and pants set with red numerals and metallic silver accents, worn during home games in the playoffs. The "Iron" uniform has become a fan favorite and is associated with the franchise's postseason identity.

===Mascot===
{{nihongo|'''Akakiba'''|赤牙|Red Fang}} is the Direwolves' official mascot—a red-furred wolf wearing a festival [[jinbaori]] (a traditional surcoat worn over armor, often bearing a clan crest). The jinbaori features the Direwolves' crest on the back and is styled in the team's red-and-black color scheme. Akakiba performs at all home games, leading the crowd in the "Howl"—a synchronized howl that the Okami crowd performs after every Direwolves defensive stop.

The mascot's design reflects the franchise's connection to the {{nihongo|[[Cult of Iron]]|鉄身信仰|Tesshin-shinkō}} and the warrior heritage of the {{nihongo|Forge State|鍛冶国家|Kaji Kokka}}. The jinbaori, a garment historically worn by samurai over their armor, symbolizes the concept of the player as warrior and the field as battlefield—a central metaphor in the Direwolves' branding.

===Fan culture===
The Direwolves' fan base is among the most passionate in the X-League. The official supporters' group, the {{nihongo|'''Red Pack'''|赤群|Aka-gumi}}, occupies the north end of Okami National Stadium and leads coordinated chants, drumming, and the "Howl" throughout games. The Red Pack's visual identity—red banners, wolf masks, and jinbaori replicas—has become iconic in Okaiken sports culture.

The Direwolves–Ironblades rivalry is the most intense in the X-League. Fans refer to the annual matchup as the {{nihongo|'''Iron War'''|鉄戦|Tetsusen}}, and the week preceding the game is marked by fan events, media coverage, and a palpable tension in both cities. The rivalry has occasionally produced crowd disturbances, particularly after the 2022 Rice Bowl loss, when Red Pack members clashed with Ironblades supporters outside Toyozawa Dome.

==Stadium==
{{Main|Okami National Stadium}}
The Direwolves have played their home games at [[Okami National Stadium]] since the facility opened in 2011. The stadium seats 68,000 for Lurandian Football games and is known for its intense atmosphere—the Red Pack's drumming and chanting create a wall of sound that visiting teams have described as disorienting. The stadium's location in central Okami and its proximity to the [[Okami Metro]] Chuo Line make it one of the most accessible venues in the X-League.

Prior to 2011, the Direwolves played at [[Okami Municipal Stadium]], a 42,000-seat facility that was demolished in 2013 to make way for a mixed-use development.

==Rivalries==
===Toyozawa Ironblades===
{{Main|Iron War (X-League rivalry)}}
The Direwolves' rivalry with the [[Toyozawa Ironblades]] is the most significant in the X-League and one of the most intense in all of Okaiken sport. Known as the {{nihongo|'''Iron War'''|鉄戦|Tetsusen}}, the rivalry is defined by the decade-long clash between Direwolves defensive end [[Kengo Shibata]] and Ironblades quarterback [[Ryuichi Nakada]]. The annual matchup is the most-watched regular season broadcast in Okaiken television history, with the 2024 meeting drawing 52.3 million viewers.

The rivalry has produced several iconic moments, including the 2015 Rice Bowl (Direwolves 17, Ironblades 14), the 2020 Rice Bowl (Direwolves 34, Ironblades 14), and the 2022 Rice Bowl (Ironblades 24, Direwolves 21). The Ironblades lead the all-time series 28–26, but the Direwolves hold a 3–2 advantage in Rice Bowl matchups.

===Saitama Samurai===
The rivalry with the [[Saitama Samurai]] is the Direwolves' second-most significant, rooted in the geographic proximity of Okami and Soma and the contrast between the Direwolves' defense-first identity and the Samurai's offensive firepower. The 2024 Rice Bowl—Direwolves 27, Samurai 20—intensified the rivalry, as the Samurai had defeated the Direwolves in the 2019 Rice Bowl.

